                <pb facs="1758" n="1718" xml:id="pg1718"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">684A.86/12–1354: Despatch</note>
                <head>No. 932<lb/>The Ambassador in Syria (Moose) to the Department of State </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Damascus</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1954-12-13"
                        >December 13, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">Top Secret<lb/>No. 227</p>
                <list type="references">
                    <item>Ref: Dept’s CA–3378, November 22, 1954. </item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <item>Subject:</item>
                    <item>Arab-Israeli Settlement</item>
                </list>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Summary</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The Embassy is in general agreement with the specific objectives of an
                    Arab-Israeli settlement set forth in the Department’s CA3378. Syria is not at
                    present in a position to be influential on this problem, and in any case is
                    unlikely to be attracted by the items listed in CA–3378 as inducements to
                    undertake negotiations. The most effective inducements for Syria to undertake
                    discussions would be (1) prior settlement with Israel by Jordan as well as
                    Egypt; and (2) evidence that Israel will negotiate in good faith and recognizes
                    that she must make some concessions in order to reach a settlement. On the
                    question of timing, it would appear advisable not to broach the subject except
                    with the United Kingdom until there has been time to evaluate (a) the
                    consequences of the execution of the Moslem Brethren in Egypt; and (b) the
                    degree of progress on area defense arrangements made during the Turkish Prime
                    Minister’s forthcoming visit to Iraq. Because of the complexity of the problem,
                    and the harm to United States objectives if an attempt at settlement should
                    fail, it is believed that, once a course of action along the lines of CA–3378
                    has been adopted, the United States, in conjunction with the United Kingdom,
                    should be prepared to employ “unusual measures” if necessary to attain our
                    objective.</p>
                <p rendition="#right">[Here follows discussion in detail.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> James S. Moose, Jr. </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
